tools.description:
Run JavaScript that calls tools, with loops, conditions, and parallelism. The source is raw JavaScript with top-level `await`, not JSON or a Markdown fence. Each Script is a fresh, isolated evaluation: no imports, `require`, Node globals, or direct filesystem or network access; every external action goes through `tools`.

Working rules:
- One nested call per command or file, as many per Script as the work needs: a fused call hides which part failed, a Script per call wastes turns. Start independent calls (reads, searches, shell commands alike) together in one `Promise.allSettled` so they overlap, check every result, and label what you emit:
    const paths = ['/abs/a.ts', '/abs/b.ts'];
    const results = await Promise.allSettled(paths.map((file_path) => tools.Read({ file_path })));
    results.forEach((r, i) => text(`## ${paths[i]}\n${r.status === 'fulfilled' ? r.value : String(r.reason)}`));
- Await dependent calls in order and every call whose outcome matters: an unawaited call can fail unseen.
- At most 128 nested calls may be in flight, with inputs under 1 MiB in total; more fails the Script, so batch large fan-outs.
- Keep intermediate data in the program and emit what you need to decide or report (lines, fields, paths, exit codes, errors), in full when the details matter; filter, slice, or count output of unknown size first, but never filter away what could change the conclusion.

Invocation:
- `tools` holds exactly the tools the nested tool reference and any "Script bindings loaded" reminder declare, called as `tools.<path>(input)`; call your top-level tools directly, never through `tools`.
- Bindings are fixed when a Script starts; a tool added later is callable from the next Script.
- Any text of more than one line that a call takes (a file body, a patch, replacement text, a multi-line command) goes in an input block before the program, and the program reads it as the string `inputs.<name>`. A body is the exact text between its opener line and the first line that equals its tag: pick a tag no body line uses, and leave an empty line before the tag line when the body must end in a newline. Input blocks and the `// @script:` line lead the source, in any order:
// @input: note <<'EOF'
`${name}` said "it's done" in C:\temp
EOF
text(inputs.note)
- A nested result over 5 MiB rejects; its declaration says how to ask for less.

Outputs:
- Only `text()`, `image()`, and `notify()` reach you; every other value stays in the program.
- `text()` and `console.*` show an Execute result as `exit N`, its other fields, then raw output (code gets the object); other objects and arrays are JSON-encoded on one line; strings pass through and other values are stringified.
- `image(value)` shows one image, e.g. `image(await tools.Read({ file_path: '/abs/shot.png' }))`; image data in `text()` becomes a placeholder.
- At most 1024 output events (`text()`, `image()`, `notify()`) and 5 MiB per Script, images included.
- All `text()` in one Script share a 64 KiB budget; a nested result echoed verbatim is also cut to its tool's budget (32 KiB or more), except Read text, which is limited only by the shared 64 KiB. A cut keeps head and tail around a notice naming its log section (`## text N` or `## rN`); once the budget is spent, later `text()` goes only to the log, so emit conclusions before bulk data.
- Output reaches you with the next Script or WaitForScript result (`running` ones included) or the message reporting the Script's end; `notify(message)` also arrives alone while no call waits on the Script.
- The result ends with a status line (status, elapsed time, log file if any), then any failure or cancellation details. The log keeps every nested result except text Reads; read or search it in a later Script instead of rerunning a slow command.

Lifecycle:
- A Script call returns when the Script finishes, or after 5 minutes with `status: "running"` while the Script keeps going; a running result may add a progress line before its status.
- Put `// @script: {"startInBackground": true}` before the program to get the toolCallId back at launch when the next step does not need the result yet (a long build, server, or test suite); collect it with WaitForScript.
- `running` means the Script is still going; never report that work as finished. What to do next depends on this session, below.

Errors:
- A nested call's rejection starts with the call as written (`tools.Read: ...`). Wrap calls that may fail (optional-file reads) in `Promise.allSettled` or try/catch so one failure does not end the Script.
- An uncaught error fails the Script, quoting the failing statement; the status line names the last nested call that settled. Throw when a precondition fails instead of reporting success.

- This session does not wait for a running Script to finish: if you end your turn while one runs, you get one reminder, and ending your turn again stops it and discards its result. Before you finish, call WaitForScript with its toolCallId until it reports a terminal status, or stop it with `WaitForScript({ toolCallId, action: "kill" })` if it is no longer needed.

Nested tool reference:

declare const tools: {
  // Run one shell command per call. A command of more than one line is an input block that is the whole command: `tools.Execute({ command: inputs.cmd, ... })`; an inline program is a heredoc inside it (`python3 - <<'PY' … PY`), under a block tag no body line uses. Each call is a fresh process in the session working directory (`% pwd`); cd, exports, sourced files, and shell functions do not reach the next call, so a setup-only call does nothing. Chain dependent steps that need no decision in between, such as build then test, with the chain operator the shell notes below give. Independent commands are separate calls in one `Promise.allSettled`, never joined into one command: they run concurrently, and each resolves with its own `exitCode` and `output`.
  // 
  // When a command depends on a repository's working directory, explicitly target that repository in the same call using an inline cd <absolute-repo>, git -C <absolute-repo> ..., or an explicit project-file flag. Never run a bare repository-sensitive command from another directory.
  // 
  // Double-quote paths containing spaces or shell metacharacters such as ( ) [ ]. Commands that wait for input are unsupported and will hang; use non-interactive flags. Never modify ~/.factory/artifacts; it is reserved for system-generated output.
  // 
  // Resolves for any exit code with `output` and `exitCode`. Emit `text(r)` for an exit-code header, other result fields, then raw output. Filter, slice, or count `output` in JavaScript first when its size is unknown, keeping the exit code and other result fields in what you emit. When output may be large, redirect it to a file in the same command (`cmd > <file> 2>&1`) and Read or search that file in the same Script; do not pipe it into `head`, `tail`, or `grep`, which hides the command's exit code. Commands are killed at `timeout`; raise it for long builds and test suites. A timeout resolves with `exitCode: null`, `signal: 'TIMEOUT'`, and the output captured before the kill. Output too large to return rejects and names the log file to Read in slices.
  // 
  // To keep a job running after the call or Script ends, use `fireAndForget: true`, not shell `&` or a `setsid`/`$!` recipe. A running job returns launch text in `output`, `exitCode: null`, and `pid`/`outputFile`; actual command output goes to `outputFile`, not the launch text. If the command finishes during launch, the result instead returns real command output and exit status inline. Read the returned log and check the returned PID as needed. Stop long-running jobs by the returned PID when done. The Script closing line counts managed background Execute jobs still running in this session. Processes a normal command leaves running, such as `cmd &`, are stopped when the call returns; `stoppedProcesses` in the result counts them for programs. Do not use `pgrep -f` or `pkill -f`: they can match the shell running the command.
  // 
  // Commands run in a POSIX shell inside WSL (source .venv/bin/activate && pytest). Keep dependencies and build artifacts on Linux paths under /home; use /mnt/c only when the user supplied a Windows path or the task requires one, because it crosses the VM boundary, is far slower, and Windows file locks there can break installs. Windows-edited scripts may carry CRLF line endings (symptom: "$'\r': command not found"); fix with dos2unix. Interop with the Windows side via cmd.exe /c or powershell.exe -Command (environment does not cross the boundary) and translate paths with wslpath -w / -u. A heredoc replaces piped stdin, so a program supplied by heredoc cannot also read piped input; pass the program as an argument (python3 -c, node -e) or a script file.
  // 
  // Git: never push or rewrite published history unless the user asks. Never change or override the configured Git author/committer identity, including via git config, command-scoped -c user.*, or GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_* environment variables. Use the identity already configured by the environment. If Git reports a missing author identity, stop and report an environment configuration failure. Before committing or pushing, review the staged diff and status for unintended changes. Avoid interactive flags (-i) and hook bypasses (--no-verify). Match the style of recent commit messages. Include `Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>` in commit messages. Pass multi-line commit messages through a safe quoting mechanism (on POSIX, a quoted heredoc) rather than embedding raw newlines. If a pre-commit hook modifies files, amend the commit once to include them. When creating a pull request, describe everything the branch adds relative to the base (not just the latest commit) and target the repository's default branch unless told otherwise.
  // 
  // Read files with the Read tool rather than cat, head, tail, sed, or nl: Read takes offset and limit for line ranges, and several Read calls can go in one Script.
  // 
  // `riskLevel` is required; a call without it is rejected.
  // LOW: Commands that are strictly read-only, have no meaningful side effects, and have no credible disclosure or security impact. Examples: ls, cat, git status, git diff, SELECT-only queries, authenticated reads that only observe state.
  // MEDIUM: Commands with bounded, understood effects that are straightforward to reverse or reproduce and do not present a credible risk of severe harm. Examples: writing files outside system directories, npm install, git commit, builds and test runs, scoped deletion of generated or agent-created files.
  // HIGH: Commands with a credible possibility of severe or hard-to-recover harm. This includes destructive effects, sensitive-data disclosure, privilege or security-control changes, untrusted code execution, consequential external actions, and similarly serious risks not explicitly listed. Examples: sudo, rm -rf or other broad deletion, anything that discards uncommitted work (git reset --hard, checkout over dirty paths), git push, curl | sh, uploads, destructive database or environment resets, exposing ports, printing or moving secrets, transmitting PII to external endpoints.
  // For chained commands, use the highest risk of any subcommand that can execute. Shell syntax such as pipes, cd, grep, head, and stream merges like 2>&1 does not increase risk by itself.
  // A redirect to /dev/null does not increase risk, but any redirect to a file is a write: creating a new file should be at least MEDIUM (and may be HIGH depending on what or where it writes), and overwriting or appending to an existing file should be classified like any other modification of that target.
  // The risk must be supported by the command or context, not merely hypothetical. User authorization does not lower the risk level.
  // Parameters:
  // - summary: REQUIRED: A Sentence cased summary of what the command is about, 3-8 words long. …
  // - command: The command to execute
  // - timeout: Timeout in seconds (default: 60)
  // - riskLevel: REQUIRED: Choose the appropriate risk level for this command. Classify the command by its plausible real-world consequences, considering severity, scope, reversibility, trust, and security boundaries. …
  // - fireAndForget: Run command in background without waiting for completion. After start, note the printed PID and temp log path, run `sleep <seconds>` and Read that file to inspect output, and stop the process later with `kill <pid>`.
  Execute(input: { summary: string; command: string; timeout?: number; riskLevel: "low" | "medium" | "high"; fireAndForget?: boolean; }): Promise<{ output: string; exitCode: number | null; signal?: string; stoppedProcesses?: number; pid?: number; outputFile?: string; }>;
  // Advanced file path search using glob patterns with multiple pattern support and exclusions.
  // Uses ripgrep for high-performance file pattern matching.
  // Supports:
  // - Multiple inclusion patterns (OR logic)
  // - Exclusion patterns to filter out unwanted files
  // Common patterns:
  // - "*.ext" - all files with extension
  // - "**/*.ext" - all files with extension in any subdirectory
  // - "dir/**/*" - all files under directory
  // - "{*.js,*.ts}" - multiple extensions
  // - "!node_modules/**" - exclude pattern
  // 
  // Returns a list of matched file paths.
  // Parameters:
  // - patterns: A glob pattern string or array of glob patterns to match file paths. Examples: "*.js", ["*.js", "*.ts"] for JavaScript and TypeScript files, ["src/**/*.tsx"] for React components in src, ["**/*.test.*"] for all test files.
  // - excludePatterns: A glob pattern string or array of glob patterns to exclude from results. Example: "node_modules/**", ["node_modules/**", "dist/**", "*.min.js"]
  // - folder: Absolute path to the directory to search in. If not specified, searches in the current working directory.
  Glob(input: { patterns: string | Array<string>; excludePatterns?: string | Array<string>; folder?: string; }): Promise<Array<string>>;
  // High-performance file content search using ripgrep. Wrapper around ripgrep with comprehensive parameter support.
  // 
  // Supports ripgrep parameters:
  // - Pattern matching with regex support
  // - File type filtering (--type js, --type py, etc.)
  // - Glob pattern filtering (--glob "*.js")
  // - Case-insensitive search (-i)
  // - Context lines (-A, -B, -C for after/before/around context)
  // - Line numbers (-n)
  // - Multiline mode (-U --multiline-dotall)
  // - Custom search directories
  // 
  // Output modes:
  // - file_paths: Returns only matching file paths (default, fast)
  // - content: Returns matching lines with optional context, line numbers, and formatting
  // 
  // Returns search results based on the selected output mode.
  // Parameters:
  // - pattern: A search pattern to match in file contents. Can be a literal string or a regular expression. Supports ripgrep regex syntax.
  // - path: Absolute path to a file or directory to search in. If not specified, searches in the current working directory.
  // - glob_pattern: Glob pattern to filter files. Example: "*.js" for JavaScript files, "**/*.tsx" for React components. Maps to ripgrep --glob parameter.
  // - output_mode: Output format: "file_paths" returns only matching file paths, "content" returns matching lines with context. Content mode supports -A/-B/-C context, -n line numbers, head_limit.
  // - case_insensitive: Perform case-insensitive matching (ripgrep -i flag).
  // - type: Ripgrep file type filter for common file types (ripgrep --type flag). Examples: "js" for JavaScript, "py" for Python, "rust" for Rust, "cpp" for C++.
  // - context_before: Number of lines to show before each match (ripgrep -B flag). Only works with output_mode="content".
  // - context_after: Number of lines to show after each match (ripgrep -A flag). Only works with output_mode="content".
  // - context: Number of lines to show before and after each match (ripgrep -C flag). Only works with output_mode="content".
  // - line_numbers: Show line numbers in output (ripgrep -n flag). Only works with output_mode="content".
  // - head_limit: Limit output to first N lines/entries. Works with both output modes.
  // - multiline: Enable multiline mode where . matches newlines and patterns can span lines (ripgrep -U --multiline-dotall).
  // - fixed_string: Treat the pattern as a literal string instead of a regular expression (ripgrep -F flag). Useful for searching special characters like ?, *, etc.
  Grep(input: { pattern: string; path?: string; glob_pattern?: string; output_mode?: "file_paths" | "content"; case_insensitive?: boolean; type?: string; context_before?: number; context_after?: number; context?: number; line_numbers?: boolean; head_limit?: number; multiline?: boolean; fixed_string?: boolean; } & { output_mode: 'content' }): Promise<Array<{ path: string; line: number; text: string; kind: "match" | "context"; }>>;
  Grep(input: { pattern: string; path?: string; glob_pattern?: string; output_mode?: "file_paths" | "content"; case_insensitive?: boolean; type?: string; context_before?: number; context_after?: number; context?: number; line_numbers?: boolean; head_limit?: number; multiline?: boolean; fixed_string?: boolean; }): Promise<Array<string>>;
  // List the contents of a directory with optional pattern-based filtering.
  // Prefer usage of 'Grep' and 'Glob' tools, for more targeted searches.
  // Supports ignore patterns to exclude unwanted files and directories.
  // Parameters:
  // - directory_path: The absolute path to the directory to list (must be absolute, not relative). Defaults to current working directory if not provided.
  // - ignorePatterns: Array of glob patterns to ignore when listing files and directories. Example: ["node_modules/**", "*.log", ".git/**"]
  LS(input: { directory_path?: string; ignorePatterns?: Array<string>; }): Promise<Array<{ name: string; type: "file" | "directory" | "symlink" | "other"; size: number; }>>;
  // Resolves with the whole text file by default, with no character or line cap; a file or slice too large to return rejects, so read it in slices with `offset` and `limit`. Images return an image block, PDFs content blocks.
  // Parameters:
  // - file_path: The absolute path to the file to read (must be absolute, not relative)
  // - offset: The line number to start reading from (1-based, defaults to 1)
  // - limit: Maximum number of lines to read. Omit to read through the end of the file.
  // - line_numbers: Prefix each returned line with its line number as `12→text`. Ask for this when you need to cite, page, or check a line, instead of re-reading the file through cat -n, nl or sed. Only applicable when reading text files.
  // - image_quality: Image compression quality. "default" uses standard compression (~200KB), "high" uses higher fidelity (~1MB). Only applicable when reading image files.
  Read(input: { file_path: string; offset?: number; limit?: number; line_numbers?: boolean; image_quality?: "default" | "high"; }): Promise<string | { id?: string; type: "image"; source: { type: "base64"; data: string; mediaType: "image/jpeg" | "image/png" | "image/gif" | "image/webp"; }; generated?: boolean; } | Array<{ id?: string; type: "text"; text: string; } | { id?: string; type: "document"; source: { type: "base64"; mediaType: "application/pdf"; data: string; parsedData?: string; name?: string; path?: string; } | { type: "text"; mediaType: "text/plain"; data: string; name?: string; mime?: string; }; }>>;
  // Activate one skill by exact name from the latest system reminder. Use a relevant skill proactively, but never invent a name or reactivate an active skill.
  // Parameters:
  // - skill: The skill name to execute
  Skill(input: { skill: string; }): Promise<string>;
};
